🌸 Interactive Petal Blast:-
A lightweight, high-performance HTML5 Canvas interaction that creates a burst of cherry blossom petals on every click. 
This script features a constant directional wind force and a "landing" mechanic where petals settle on the floor before fading away.✨ Features: Performance-First: Driven by requestAnimationFrame and a lightweight Particle class. 
This is Physics-Based: Includes gravity, air resistance (wobble), and constant wind. Landing Logic: Petals detect the bottom of the viewport and "land" before vanishing.
Directional Wind: A steady breeze pushes particles from Left to Right. 
Zero Dependencies: Pure Vanilla JavaScript, CSS, and HTML.🚀 Quick Start: To use this effect, simply copy the code into an .html file or integrate the components into your existing project.1. The HTML. The effect requires a single canvas element. 
It is recommended to place this at the end of your <body>.HTML<canvas id="petal-canvas"></canvas>

The CSS: Ensure the canvas stays fixed and doesn't interfere with your buttons or links. CSS #petal-canvas {
    position: fixed;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    pointer-events: none; /* Crucial: allows clicking on links behind the canvas */
    z-index: 9999;
}
🧠 Core Logic Particle System:-
Uses an array of objects to track the $x, y$ coordinates, rotation, and opacity of each petal.Physics: * Gravity: A constant downward force applied to vertical velocity.Wind: A fixed horizontal offset ($+x$) applied every frame to simulate a left-to-right breeze.Wobble: Math.sin() creates a natural side-to-side "drifting" effect.Collision: Detects when a petal's $y$-position reaches the bottom of the screen. Upon "landing," vertical movement stops, and a fade-out timer begins. Memory Management: Automatically removes (splices) particles from the array once they are invisible or blown off-screen to ensure 60FPS performance.

🛠️ Customization:-
You can easily tweak the behavior by modifying the config object at the top of the script: PropertyDescriptionDefaultpetalCountNumber of petals spawned per click.20 gravity. How fast petals fall (0.1) windStrengthForce pushing petals to the right. (1.2) fade Speed: How fast petals disappear once landed(.0.008)colorsHSL Hue values (340=Pink, 15=Orange).[340, 350, 0, 15]🧠 How it Works. The system uses a Particle Class to manage the lifecycle of each petal:Instantiation: On mousedown, an array of Petal objects is created at the cursor coordinates. 
Update Loop: Each frame, the particle calculates its new $(x, y)$ position based on speed, gravity, and the constant wind force. Collision: When $y$ reaches window.innerHeight, the isLanded flag is set to true, stopping vertical movement.
Garbage Collection: Once opacity reaches 0 or the petal drifts off-screen ($x > width$), it is spliced from the array to save memory.
